{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"coprime ideals coprime ideals","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md","fileName":"Coprime Ideals.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Coprime Ideals","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 19:40\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"coprime ideals coprime ideals","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md","fileName":"Coprime Ideals.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Coprime Ideals","heading":"Coprime Ideals","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Two ideals I and J are coprime when I + J is the whole ring. Equivalently, there are elements a ∈ I and b ∈ J with a + b = 1.\n\nFor coprime ideals, intersection and product agree: I ∩ J = IJ. Pairwise coprimality is also the condition that makes the canonical map to a product of quotient rings surjective, a ring-level form of the Chinese remainder principle.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"coprime ideals coprime ideals","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Coprime Ideals.md","fileName":"Coprime Ideals.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Coprime Ideals","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[commutativealg